(function(){
  const P = window.TWOFY_PEDIA = window.TWOFY_PEDIA || {};
  function set(key, f) { P[key] = Object.assign(P[key] || {}, f); }
  set("lm:liberty", {extract:"A copper colossus on Liberty Island in New York Harbor, dedicated in 1886 as a gift from France. Bartholdi designed the figure; Eiffel designed the iron armature. The copper skin is only 2.4 millimeters thick and has weathered to green. Emma Lazarus's sonnet was added later, when the statue had already become an immigration emblem rather than a Franco-American friendship gift alone. The torch was replaced in the 1980s restoration. Ferries still run from Battery Park and New Jersey."});
  set("lm:empire", {extract:"An Art Deco skyscraper on Fifth Avenue in Midtown Manhattan, completed in 1931. It was finished in one year and 45 days during the Depression, a stack of limestone and steel that held the world's-tallest title for four decades. King Kong (1933) did more for its fame than any brochure. The mast was built for airships that never docked. Lights on the crown still change color for civic occasions. The 102nd-floor observatory is a small room with a long view."});
  set("lm:ellis", {extract:"The principal federal immigration station in New York Harbor from 1892 to 1954. Steerage passengers were the majority of those inspected; first and second class often cleared on the ship. A hospital, a kitchen that could serve 20,000 meals a day at peak, and a great registry hall processed hope and rejection. The island was expanded with landfill, some of it from subway excavations. After 1954 it sat empty until restoration. The museum occupies the main building."});
  set("lm:golden", {extract:"A suspension bridge across the Golden Gate strait between San Francisco and Marin County, opened in 1937. Chief engineer Joseph Strauss had never built a bridge this large; the steel, fog, and International Orange paint did the rest. The main cables hold more than 27,000 wires each. It opened in 1937 after men died in the building of it — and after a safety net saved many others. The towers sit on bedrock and caissons in a tidal strait famous for wind. It is still a working highway, not a museum."});
  set("lm:alcatraz", {extract:"A rocky island in San Francisco Bay used as a lighthouse, military post, and federal penitentiary from 1934 to 1963. The prison closed in 1963 because it cost too much to supply on an island in cold current. Native activists occupied it from 1969 to 1971, a protest that helped shift federal policy. Before the penitentiary it was a military fort and a lighthouse site. The cells are small; the view of San Francisco is insultingly beautiful. No confirmed escapees are known to have survived the swim, though the 1962 attempt remains a story."});
  set("lm:hollywood", {extract:"White block letters on Mount Lee above Los Angeles, first erected in 1923 as HOLLYWOODLAND and shortened in 1949. Each letter is about 45 feet tall. The sign was a real-estate advertisement that outlived the development. It sits on Mount Lee above a basin that is not a town called Hollywood so much as a district and a metonym. Restoration in 1978 replaced crumbling letters. Hikers still trespass; helicopters still film it. The view from Griffith Observatory is the civic postcard."});
  set("lm:needle", {extract:"An observation tower built for the 1962 World's Fair in Seattle. Built for the 1962 World's Fair, it was meant to look like the future and still does, in a 1960s way. Elevators travel at about 10 miles an hour to a saucer with a revolving restaurant and a 360-degree view of Sound, city, and Rainier when the mountain is out. The structure is an earthquake-designed tripod. It is 605 feet tall. Seattle's skyline grew around it rather than replacing it."});
  set("lm:rushmore", {extract:"A granite sculpture in the Black Hills of South Dakota showing Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt, and Lincoln. The carved heads are about 60 feet tall. Gutzon Borglum and his son Lincoln directed the work, using dynamite and jackhammers, from 1927 to 1941. Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt, and Lincoln were chosen as a national program in granite. The unfinished Hall of Records sits behind the heads. The sculpture stands in the Black Hills, on land included in the 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty with the Sioux."});
  set("lm:arch", {extract:"A stainless-steel catenary arch on the Mississippi riverfront in St. Eero Saarinen's stainless catenary is 630 feet high and 630 feet wide, a monument to Jeffersonian expansion on a river that was the old western door. Capsules climb inside the legs to a cramped observation room. Each leg sits on bedrock 44 feet down. It opened in 1965. The old courthouse where Dred Scott was heard stands a few blocks west. The river still floods the grounds on a schedule."});
  set("lm:hoover", {extract:"A concrete arch-gravity dam in Black Canyon on the Colorado River, completed in 1936. More than 3 million cubic yards of concrete were placed in a canyon that had to be diverted first. The dam tamed the Colorado for Los Angeles, Phoenix, and farms, and created Lake Mead — which has since dropped as the river is over-allocated. Generators still feed the southwest grid. About a hundred people died in the building. The Art Deco intake towers are as designed as the engineering."});
  set("lm:grandcyn", {extract:"A canyon of the Colorado River in northern Arizona, up to eighteen miles wide and more than a mile deep. John Wesley Powell ran the river in 1869, a one-armed Civil War veteran in wooden boats. Nearly two billion years of rock are stacked in the walls. The Havasupai live in a side canyon. Rim-to-river is a long, hot walk."});
  set("lm:delicate", {extract:"A freestanding sandstone arch in Arches National Park near Moab, Utah. It stands 52 feet high and appears on Utah license plates, a free-standing remnant of a fin of Entrada sandstone. The hike from the viewpoint is a slickrock walk with a wide view of the park. Arches National Park was established in 1971; the arch itself is far older. Other arches in the park have collapsed in living memory, a reminder that this one is temporary on geologic time."});
  set("lm:shiprock", {extract:"A volcanic neck on the Navajo Nation in northwest New Mexico, rising about 1,500 feet from the plain. The neck is the remnant of a volcanic pipe about 27 million years old, with radiating dikes like a stone compass. In Diné it is Tsé Bitʼaʼí, the winged rock, and climbing is not permitted. It rises about 1,500 feet from the plain of the Navajo Nation. Navajo Agricultural Products and oil wells occupy the same horizon. Storm light makes it look like a ship; midday makes it look like a factory of rock."});
  set("lm:totempole", {extract:"A slender sandstone butte in Monument Valley on the Utah–Arizona line. Monument Valley remains Navajo Tribal Park land, a plateau of buttes that John Ford used as a definition of the American West. The Totem Pole is a slender remnant, often climbed in films and no longer climbed in life without permission. Dust, red sand, and an enormous sky are the products. Goulding's Lodge is the old trading post. The valley sits on the Utah–Arizona line as if states were an afterthought."});
  set("lm:yosemite", {extract:"A granite monolith at the west end of Yosemite Valley, California, rising about 3,000 feet from the valley floor. The valley was protected as a state grant in 1864, before the national park. El Capitan's Nose is a landmark of big-wall climbing; the granite is a pluton polished by ice. Waterfalls in spring become streaks in autumn. Ahwahneechee people were pushed out for the park idea. Traffic in the valley in summer is a different kind of wilderness problem. Half Dome is the other postcard, with cables on the spine."});
  set("lm:yellowstone", {extract:"A cone geyser in Yellowstone National Park, Wyoming. It was the first national park in the world, in 1872, sitting on a volcanic caldera still active. Old Faithful's interval is roughly 60 to 110 minutes, not a clock. Bison jam the roads; wolves, restored in the 1990s, changed the rivers by changing the elk. Winter is the serious season. Geysers, mud pots, and a lake at altitude make it a hydrothermal freak. The park spills into Idaho and Montana."});
  set("lm:devilstower", {extract:"A phonolite butte in northeastern Wyoming rising 867 feet from base to summit. It was the first U.S. national monument, in 1906, a phonolite butte with columnar joints that look like a pipe organ. Several Plains nations hold it sacred and ask climbers to stay off in June. Close Encounters of the Third Kind put it on a different kind of map. Prairie dogs occupy the base. The Belle Fourche River loops around it. The summit is a plateau of rock, not a spire."});
  set("lm:zion", {extract:"A park in southwestern Utah where the Virgin River has cut Navajo sandstone cliffs more than 2,000 feet high. The Virgin River still cuts Navajo sandstone in a canyon that was once called Mukuntuweap. Shuttle buses now replace summer cars in the main canyon. Angels Landing is a chain-assisted ridge with a drop on both sides. Flash floods in the Narrows are the real hazard. The park is in the southwest corner of Utah, a hinge of the Colorado Plateau. Cottonwoods go gold in autumn against red walls."});
  set("lm:bryce", {extract:"Natural amphitheaters on the Paunsaugunt Plateau in southern Utah, packed with hoodoos of pink and orange Claron limestone. Ebenezer Bryce said it was a hell of a place to lose a cow. The hoodoos are carved from pink Claron limestone in amphitheaters, not a single canyon. At 8,000 feet the air is thin and the nights are cold even in summer. The rim walks are easy; the wall descents are knees and switchbacks. It is smaller than people expect and stranger. The light at sunrise is the reason for the 5 a.m. alarm."});
  set("lm:arches", {extract:"A high-desert park north of Moab, Utah, with more than 2,000 documented sandstone arches among fins, windows, and balanced rocks. Landscape Arch is one of the longest natural spans on earth. Wall Arch collapsed in 2008. Fins of Entrada sandstone, windows, and balanced rocks occupy a high desert north of Moab. The Fiery Furnace is a maze that requires a permit. Summers are very hot. The Colorado River runs just south of the park. Delicate Arch is the famous silhouette; the rest of the park rewards a longer stay."});
  set("lm:canyonlands", {extract:"A park in southeastern Utah at the confluence of the Green and Colorado rivers. Island in the Sky is a mesa between the Green and the Colorado; The Needles and The Maze are harder to reach. The confluence is the heart, a matching of muddy rivers in a country of grabens and white rim. Uranium roads from the 1950s still scar some benches. It is larger and quieter than Arches. Edward Abbey rangers worked this district. Water is far apart."});
  set("lm:capitolreef", {extract:"A park along the Waterpocket Fold in south-central Utah, a nearly 100-mile wrinkle of warped rock named for white sandstone domes. The Waterpocket Fold is nearly 100 miles of warped rock, a monocline named for white sandstone domes that looked like capitols to someone. Nineteenth-century Mormon orchards still fruit in Fruita, a tiny oasis of green. The Fremont people left petroglyphs. It is the least crowded of Utah's 'Mighty Five' and the most geologically odd. Flash floods own the wash roads after rain."});
  set("lm:deathval", {extract:"A desert basin in eastern California. Badwater Basin sits 282 feet below sea level, the lowest point in North America; Telescope Peak above it is over 11,000 feet. The 1913 Furnace Creek reading of 56.7 °C is often cited as the world air-temperature record. It is a park of borax wagons, moving rocks on Racetrack Playa, and a name that oversells: people live and work here. Spring superbloom years are rare and crowded. Summer is for the night."});
  set("lm:joshua", {extract:"A park where the Mojave and Colorado deserts meet in southern California. Joshua trees are yuccas, not true trees, pollinated by a moth. The park sits where Mojave and Colorado deserts meet: branched yuccas and monzogranite boulders in the higher west, cholla and creosote in the lower east. Climbers treat the boulders as a gym. It became a park in 1994. Climate change is shrinking the tree's range from the south. Night skies are still serious."});
  set("lm:sequoia", {extract:"A southern Sierra Nevada park protecting giant sequoia groves, including the General Sherman Tree, and high granite of the Great Western Divide. Giant sequoias grow only on the west slope of the Sierra Nevada; General Sherman is the largest known living single-stem tree by volume. The park, with Kings Canyon, protects groves and high granite of the Great Western Divide. Sherman is not the tallest — coast redwoods win that — but it is a pillar. Fire is part of the ecology and now a crisis. The road up is a set of curves with a view of the Central Valley's haze."});
  set("lm:redwood", {extract:"Coast redwood forest on the far north California shore. Coast redwoods can exceed 350 feet and need fog as well as rain. The park and state parks together protect a remnant of a forest that once ran the coast. Roosevelt elk graze the prairie bottoms. It is not a single grove but a strip of creek and slope on the far north California shore. Logging came within a generation of wiping the tallest stands. The trees are older than European presence in the Americas."});
  set("lm:crater", {extract:"A caldera lake in the Oregon Cascades formed after Mount Mazama collapsed. Wizard Island is a cinder cone in a lake with no streams in or out at the surface — rain and snow, then seepage. Mount Mazama collapsed about 7,700 years ago. The water is a blue that photographs poorly and looks fake in person. It is the deepest lake in the United States at 1,943 feet. Snow closes the rim road much of the year. Klamath and Modoc stories remember the mountain as a person."});
  set("lm:olympic", {extract:"A park on Washington's Olympic Peninsula covering glaciered peaks, west-slope rainforest, and a wild Pacific coast. The park is three landscapes in one: glaciered peaks, a Hoh rainforest that can receive more than 140 inches of rain, and a wild Pacific coast of stacks and tide. The peninsula was isolated enough that some species evolved in place. Roosevelt elk graze the prairie bottoms. The rain shadow of the mountains makes Sequim almost dry. It became a park in 1938. Banana slugs are a genuine resident of the forest floor."});
  set("lm:nrcasc", {extract:"A steep, glaciated range in northern Washington. The range holds hundreds of glaciers, the densest ice in the lower 48, in a park that is more vertical than visitable. Stehekin is reached by boat. The North Cascades Highway is closed in winter. Peaks of gneiss and volcanic leftovers sit close to Canada. It is a climbers' park more than a drive-through. Wolves and wolverines still use it. The Skagit dams in the same mountains light Seattle."});
  set("lm:tetons", {extract:"The high point of the Teton Range in Wyoming at 13,775 feet. The range is young; uplift is only a few million years old, which is why there are no foothills — the fault scarp is a wall. Grand Teton is 13,775 feet. Jackson Hole was a trapping basin and is now an expensive one. Moose in willows, a snake river braiding, and a skyline that looks drawn. The park was a political fight between dude ranchers and monument-makers. Winter is for the valley floor."});
  set("lm:rocky", {extract:"A Colorado park on the Front Range spanning the Continental Divide. Trail Ridge Road tops out above 12,000 feet on alpine tundra, one of the highest paved through-roads in the United States. Elk bugle in autumn; the park straddles the Continental Divide. Estes Park is the east gate. It is close enough to Denver to be crowded. The 2013 floods rewrote some canyons. Longs Peak is the fourteener that everyone tries. Afternoon thunderstorms are a rule, not a chance."});
  set("lm:mesaverde", {extract:"A park in southwestern Colorado preserving Ancestral Pueblo cliff dwellings, including Cliff Palace, built into alcoves in the thirteenth century. Cliff Palace has more than 150 rooms, built into an alcove in the thirteenth century by Ancestral Pueblo people who later left the mesa. Drought, conflict, and migration are among the explanations. The mesa top was farm country of a vanished kind. The park was established in 1906. Many dwellings are reached by ranger tour and ladders. The name means green table, from juniper and piñon."});
  set("lm:greatsand", {extract:"The tallest dunes in North America, piled against the Sangre de Cristo Mountains in southern Colorado. Some dunes move several feet in a year, piled against the Sangre de Cristo by a trick of wind and a closed basin. Medano Creek flows at the dune foot in spring, a beach in the mountains. The park is young as a park (2004) and old as sand. Star Dune is among the tallest in North America. The sand squeaks. Nights are cold; the valley is high. It is an oddity that photographs like a different continent."});
  set("lm:blackcyn", {extract:"A narrow canyon of Precambrian rock in western Colorado. Some walls drop more than 2,000 feet with little width between them, Precambrian rock so dark the canyon stays in shadow — hence the name. The Gunnison is older than the uplift. Painted Wall is a pegmatite-striped cliff. It is not Grand Canyon scaled; it is claustrophobic and steep. The drive-to rims are easy; the inner canyon is expert country. A thin sliver of sky is the view from the river."});
  set("lm:bigbend", {extract:"A park on the Rio Grande in southwest Texas where the river bends around the Chisos Mountains. The park is larger than Rhode Island, a bend of the Rio Grande around the Chisos, desert, and a sky-island forest. It is a dark-sky reserve. Mexico is a swim away; the river is a border that wildlife ignores. Javelina, hot springs, and a long way from a hospital define a visit. It became a park in 1944. Summers are for the mountains; winters for the river. The name is the map."});
  set("lm:badlands", {extract:"Eroded buttes and mixed-grass prairie in southwestern South Dakota. The formations erode about an inch a year, exposing Oligocene fossil beds of early horses and oreodonts. Mixed-grass prairie still holds bison. The park is in southwestern South Dakota, a striped landscape next to the Pine Ridge. The name comes from Lakota and French descriptions of a place that was hard to cross. Sunsets turn the buttes a warm color. Rattlesnakes live here. The loop road is enough for most visits; a wilderness area holds the rest."});
  set("lm:acadia", {extract:"A park on Mount Desert Island and nearby Maine coast. It was the first national park east of the Mississippi, largely donated by private landowners on Mount Desert Island. Cadillac Mountain is often among the first places in the United States to see sunrise. Carriage roads of crushed rock, a gift of Rockefeller, still exclude cars. Granite, spruce, and a cold Atlantic. Fog is a season. Lobster boats work the same views. The fire of 1947 remade some of the island's forest."});
  set("lm:everglades", {extract:"A slow sheet of water moving south through sawgrass and mangrove in southern Florida. It is a sheet of water moving south through sawgrass, a river too wide to look like one, and the largest subtropical wilderness in the United States. It is UNESCO-listed. Pythons, an introduced problem, now hunt in it. The Tamiami Trail blocked sheet flow for decades; restoration is a civil-engineering epic. Mosquitoes are the air force. It is not a swamp in the cypress sense everywhere — much of it is wet prairie. Crocodiles and alligators both occur."});
  set("lm:drytort", {extract:"Coral islands west of Key West dominated by nineteenth-century Fort Jefferson, surrounded by reef and open water. Fort Jefferson was never finished, a huge brick hexagon on a coral island seventy miles west of Key West. The park is reef, wreck, and bird. Garden Key is the visit; Loggerhead is the light. Yellow fever and a prison for Dr. Mudd (Lincoln's assassin's physician) are the nineteenth-century story. Snorkel the walls. There is no freshwater except what you bring. The name is turtles without water."});
  set("lm:denali", {extract:"The highest peak in North America at 20,310 feet, in the Alaska Range. The mountain was officially renamed Denali in 2015, restoring the Koyukon name; McKinley remains a political argument. It is 20,310 feet, the highest in North America, with a vertical relief that humiliates higher Himalayan peaks from base to summit. The park is six million acres of tundra, grizzly, and braided rivers. Most visitors never leave the bus road. Climbing season is late spring. The mountain makes its own weather and hides for weeks."});
  set("lm:glacier", {extract:"A park in the northern Rockies of Montana on the Canadian border. Going-to-the-Sun Road was completed in 1932, a ledge across the divide at Logan Pass. Most of the park's named glaciers have shrunk sharply since 1900; the name is becoming historical. It meets Waterton in Canada as an international peace park. Bears, both kinds. The east side is dry and open; the west is cedar and wet. Going-to-the-Sun closes for snow more than half the year. The Blackfeet boundary is the east door."});
  set("lm:hawaii", {extract:"A park on the Island of Hawaiʻi protecting Kīlauea and Mauna Loa, two of the most active volcanoes on earth. Kīlauea has been one of the most active volcanoes on earth; Mauna Loa is the other giant of the park. Lava has added and destroyed land in living memory, including a 2018 lower-east-rift eruption. The park includes a desert of recent rock and a rainforest on older flows. Pele is a figure of deep meaning for many residents. The Chain of Craters Road dead-ends at the sea. Volcanic smog, or vog, is part of the air. It is a UNESCO site of fire and isolation."});
  set("lm:whitehouse", {extract:"The official residence and workplace of the President of the United States, on Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington, D.C. John Adams was the first president to occupy it, in 1800. The British burned it in 1814; the walls still stand inside later interiors. It is both a home and an office. The West Wing is a later addition. Public tours enter from the south. Lafayette Square is the front lawn of gatherings and protests. The address is 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue."});
  set("lm:washmon", {extract:"An Egyptian Revival obelisk on the National Mall honoring George Washington. A change of stone color marks the halt in construction during the Civil War. It was the world's tallest structure when completed in 1884, an Egyptian Revival obelisk of 555 feet. The elevator is the visit; the stairs exist. It sits on axis with the Capitol and Lincoln Memorial, the spine of the Mall. Surveys put it slightly off-center from the original plan. Lightning rods are not decorative."});
  set("lm:alamo", {extract:"A former Spanish mission in San Antonio, Texas. The mission was secularized long before 1836. The thirteen-day siege and the death of the garrison became a republic's founding myth — remember, and also simplify. It is a small church in a busy San Antonio intersection now. The Long Barrack is the better history. Tejano as well as Anglo defenders died here. The Daughters of the Republic of Texas long ran it; the state does now. The riverwalk is a block away and a different century."});
  set("lm:keywest", {extract:"A concrete buoy in Key West, Florida, marking a ceremonial southern limit of the continental United States, about ninety miles from Cuba. The buoy is a modern marker, not a colonial monument, a concrete totem for a town that is closer to Havana than to Miami in miles. U.S. Route 1 ends here. The Conch Republic seceded as a joke in 1982 and kept the joke. Hurricanes arrive. Fresh water is piped."});
  set("lm:niagara", {extract:"Three waterfalls on the Niagara River between New York and Ontario. The falls have receded several miles upstream since the last ice age and will recede more. Horseshoe Falls on the Canadian side carries most of the flow; American and Bridal Veil are the U.S. pair. A 1950 treaty splits water between power and spectacle. Ice bridges once formed; daredevils still barrel. The mist is a climate. Maid of the Mist boats wear plastic ponchos as a uniform. It is a city of motels on both banks."});
  set("lm:eiffel", {extract:"An iron lattice tower on the Champ de Mars in Paris, completed in 1889 for the Exposition Universelle. It was meant to be temporary for the 1889 Exposition Universelle and became the city's letterhead. Eiffel also built the Statue of Liberty's armature — a habit of iron. Lights sparkle on the hour at night, a later addition. Occupied Paris saw Hitler's tourist moment; the resistance cut the elevator cables. The Champ de Mars is the lawn. Wind sways the top."});
  set("lm:louvre", {extract:"A former royal palace on the Right Bank of the Seine, now the Louvre Museum. It is the most visited art museum in the world, a palace that became a public collection in 1793. Pei's glass pyramid (1989) was hated and then photographed. The Mona Lisa is smaller than people expect and more defended. The building itself is the other exhibit: medieval fortress, royal palace, ministry, museum. The Seine is at the door. Wings would take weeks. The inverted pyramid appears in a certain thriller and on the way to the Metro."});
  set("lm:notredame", {extract:"A medieval cathedral on the Île de la Cité. Construction began in the twelfth century; a 2019 fire destroyed the spire and much of the roof. Reconstruction reopened the cathedral in 2024, a national project of timber and argument. Quasimodo is fiction; the flying buttresses are not. It sits on the Île de la Cité, the old ship of the city. Kings were not crowned here (that was Reims) but national funerals were. Gargoyles drain rain. The bells have names."});
  set("lm:montstmic", {extract:"A tidal island commune in Normandy crowned by a medieval abbey. Legend dates the first oratory to the eighth century, an archangel and a bishop. The abbey is a medieval town stacked on a granite knob in tidal flats that can still isolate and drown the unwary. A causeway now reaches it; UNESCO lists the bay. Omelettes are the tourist food. It was a prison under Napoleon. The mount is both fortress and church, which is a Norman combination."});
  set("lm:arc", {extract:"A triumphal arch at the west end of the Champs-Élysées, completed in 1836. The Unknown Soldier has lain beneath it since 1921, an eternal flame that is actually maintained. Napoleon ordered the arch; it was finished in 1836. Twelve avenues radiate from the Étoile, a traffic circle of courage. The names of generals are carved; some are crossed out by history. De Gaulle walked it in 1944. You climb it for a roof of Paris. The Champs-Élysées runs to the Tuileries line."});
  set("lm:bigben", {extract:"The clock tower at the north end of the Palace of Westminster in London. Big Ben is the Great Bell, 13.7 tons; the tower is Elizabeth Tower since 2012. It has kept time for Parliament since 1859, with pauses for repair. The Palace of Westminster is a neo-Gothic rebuild after a fire. The clock faces are huge; the chimes are a signature of radio. German bombs missed it more than once. Restoration scaffolding hid it for years. The Thames is at the foot."});
  set("lm:towerbr", {extract:"A combined bascule and suspension bridge over the Thames beside the Tower of London, opened in 1894. The bascules still open for river traffic, a Victorian machine in Gothic dress opened in 1894. It is not London Bridge — that is the dull one upstream. High-level walkways were a toll crossing and then a glass floor. The Tower of London is the neighbour, older by centuries. Steam hydraulics became oil and electricity. Photographers stand where everyone stands. Fog is rarer now than in the etchings."});
  set("lm:stonehenge", {extract:"A prehistoric circle of sarsen and bluestone on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, raised in stages in the third millennium BCE. The larger sarsens came from the Marlborough Downs; the smaller bluestones from Wales, a transport problem that is still argued. Raised in stages in the third millennium BCE, it is a cemetery, a calendar, and a tourist rope-line. Solstice crowds are a modern ritual. The A303 runs too close. Aubrey holes, heel stone, and a landscape of barrows make it a complex, not a circle alone. No one was a druid here when it was new."});
  set("lm:edinburgh", {extract:"A fortress on Castle Rock, an extinct volcanic plug above Edinburgh. Castle Rock is a volcanic plug, which is why the fortress works. Mons Meg, a medieval bombard, sits on the ramparts. It has been royal residence, garrison, and prison. The Royal Mile runs downhill to Holyrood. The Tattoo is a later spectacle. Mary, Queen of Scots, and a long list of sieges occupy the rooms. The New Town is Georgian and another city. Haar, the sea fog, still arrives."});
  set("lm:giants", {extract:"About 40,000 interlocking basalt columns on the north coast of County Antrim, Northern Ireland, formed by an ancient lava flow. The columns formed as basalt cooled and shrank, a hexagonal habit of lava, about 40,000 of them on the Antrim coast. Legend says Finn MacCool built a causeway to Scotland; geology says the same flow appears at Fingal's Cave. UNESCO listed it. The sea is violent. Tour buses are the other force of nature. It is in Northern Ireland, which matters to the passport. Spray and jointed rock are the visit."});
  set("lm:colosseum", {extract:"An elliptical amphitheatre in central Rome, completed under Titus in 80 CE. It could hold an estimated 50,000 to 80,000, and could be flooded for mock sea battles in its early years. Completed under Titus in 80 CE, it is an ellipse of travertine and concrete that became a quarry, a fortress, a church property, and then a ruin-as-symbol. Cats live in the shade. The hypogeum under the floor is a machine of lifts and animals. Earthquakes took the south side. It is the emblem of Rome because it is still huge."});
  set("lm:pisa", {extract:"The campanile of Pisa Cathedral. The tilt began before the tower was finished, on soft ground under a heavy campanile. Stabilization in the 1990s reduced the lean to a safer angle. It is the bell tower of a cathedral complex on the Field of Miracles, not a standalone curiosity. Galileo dropping weights from it is a good story with a shaky source. The bells still work. White marble and green grass make the famous photograph."});
  set("lm:venice", {extract:"The cathedral of Venice on Piazza San Marco, built in a Byzantine-Italian style and faced with mosaics and eastern marbles. The Pala d'Oro is among the most famous altarpieces in Europe, a gold Byzantine wall. St Mark's is the doge's chapel become cathedral, a looted and glittering hybrid. The campanile fell in 1902 and was rebuilt as it was. Acqua alta floods the piazza on a schedule that is getting worse. Pigeons were a plague and are now managed. The four bronze horses are copies; the loot is inside. It is a church that looks like a plundered empire, because it is."});
  set("lm:duomo", {extract:"The Duomo di Milano, a vast Gothic cathedral begun in 1386. The last gate was set in 1965, nearly six centuries after the first stone in 1386. The roof is a forest of spires that can be walked, a marble mountain in the middle of a fashion city. It is among the largest churches on earth. Saint Charles Borromeo and a nail of the Crucifixion are the relics. Bombing in 1943 missed the miracle and hit the rest. The gold Madonnina at the top is the city's watch. Fog and trams at the door."});
  set("lm:vesuvius", {extract:"A stratovolcano on the Bay of Naples. Pompeii and Herculaneum were buried in a few hours of 79 CE; Pliny the Younger wrote the eyewitness letter. It is the only active volcano on the European mainland, a cone over a caldera, with Naples metro three million people in the danger zone. It last erupted in 1944. The crater hike is ash and a view of the bay. Wine grows on the slopes. Evacuation plans exist on paper. The mountain looks small until it does not."});
  set("lm:etna", {extract:"An active stratovolcano on the east coast of Sicily and the highest volcano in Europe south of the Caucasus. It has been in near-continuous eruption for thousands of years, the highest volcano in Europe south of the Caucasus, a UNESCO site. Ski lifts and lava fields share the mountain. Catania was destroyed more than once. The Sicilians treat it as a neighbour, not a spectacle. Four summit craters and countless laterals. Ash closes the airport on a whim. The name is older than the Greeks. Oranges grow below the snow."});
  set("lm:sagrada", {extract:"A basilica in Barcelona designed by Antoni Gaudí. Gaudí is buried in the crypt. Work began in 1882 and continues, a basilica of eighteen planned spires for apostles, evangelists, Mary, and Christ. Nativity and Passion façades are different centuries of style. Completion of the central tower is still underway. It is Barcelona's skyline and its argument about taste. Tourist tickets fund the stone. The interior is a forest of branched columns and colored light. It is not a cathedral — the diocese already had one."});
  set("lm:alhambra", {extract:"A palace and fortress above Granada, developed by the Nasrid emirs in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries. The Nasrid palaces are the core, stucco and water in a fortress above Granada, developed in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries. Washington Irving's Tales popularized it in English. Ferdinand and Isabella took it in 1492; the Catholic overlay is still visible. The Generalife gardens are the summer house. Tickets sell out. The view back at the Albaicín is the other half of the postcard. Fountains are the air conditioning of a dry kingdom."});
  set("lm:acropolis", {extract:"The high citadel of ancient Athens. The Parthenon was begun in 447 BCE, Pentelic marble on a limestone plateau that was already a Mycenaean fort. Elgin took a cargo of sculpture to London, an argument without end. The Erechtheion's caryatids hold a porch; one is a copy, the original in the museum. Pollution and visitors wear the rock. Restoration is a century of scaffolding. Athena's city is still named for the contest she won with an olive."});
  set("lm:santorini", {extract:"A flooded volcanic caldera in the southern Aegean. A Bronze Age eruption helped shape the present caldera, a candidate for all kinds of Atlantis stories. The inner cliffs of Thera drop to a deep bay. White houses on the lip are a 20th-century tourist vernacular as much as a tradition. Wine, seismic jitters, and cruise ships. Akrotiri, the buried town, is the serious archaeology. The light is famous because the rock is dark and the walls are lime. Donkeys and cable cars fight for the same path."});
  set("lm:neusch", {extract:"A nineteenth-century palace in the Bavarian Alps commissioned by Ludwig II, overlooking the Pöllat Gorge. Ludwig II never saw it finished. The palace is a nineteenth-century romantic castle on a crag above the Pöllat Gorge, and it became the world’s idea of a fairy-tale castle — a look later echoed by Disney. Interior rooms are few and ornate. The walk up from the valley is part of the visit. Hohenschwangau, his father’s house, sits below. Fog can make it look older than it is."});
  set("lm:brandenburg", {extract:"A neoclassical gate on Pariser Platz in Berlin, completed in 1793. The quadriga was taken to Paris by Napoleon and later returned. The gate stood in the death strip of a divided city and reopened as a crossing in 1989. It is a modest arch for so much history, sandstone and a chariot of victory. Pariser Platz is the front yard. New Year's fireworks and political rallies share it. Berlin's center of gravity moved back here after the Wall. The Reichstag is a short walk."});
  set("lm:cologne", {extract:"A High Gothic cathedral on the Rhine in Cologne. Medieval cranes stood on the unfinished nave for centuries; the cathedral was completed in 1880 to medieval plans, a German national project. It houses the Shrine of the Three Kings. Allied bombing flattened the city and left the Dom standing, black with soot now cleaned in patches. The bells include Peter, one of the largest swinging bells. It sits on the Rhine as a navigation mark. The square is a station exit."});
  set("lm:matterhorn", {extract:"A 4,478-meter pyramidal peak on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt. Four of the first ascent party died on the way down in 1865, a Victorian scandal of ropes and glory. The pyramidal peak is 4,478 meters on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt, a town that banned cars and invented a kind of alpine tourism. The Hornli ridge is the classic route. It looks the same from a thousand postcards because the shape is that simple. Glaciers recede. The train to Gornergrat is the easy summit."});
  set("lm:atomium", {extract:"A Brussels landmark built for Expo 58. Each sphere is 18 meters across, an iron crystal magnified 165 billion times, built for Expo 58 and kept because Brussels liked the joke. A restaurant occupies the top. Escalators in tubes connect the atoms. It was meant to be temporary, like Eiffel. Heysel plateau is not the old city. At night it is a science-fiction lamp. The expo dreamed of peace and isotopes. The structure still leaks a little, as icons do."});
  set("lm:keukenhof", {extract:"A seasonal display garden at Lisse in South Holland. The display garden opened to the public in 1949, a seasonal factory of tulips at Lisse in the bulb district of South Holland. It is open only in spring. Millions of bulbs, a castle, and a very Dutch genius for controlled nature. The Keukenhof is not wild — it is horticulture as spectacle. Windmills in the polder are working cousins. Tour buses from Amsterdam are the pollinators. The name means kitchen garden, from a castle's herbs."});
  set("lm:sagrada2", {extract:"A park on Carmel Hill in Barcelona designed by Antoni Gaudí for Eusebi Güell, with mosaic benches and a terrace over the city. Güell's housing estate was never fully built; the park became municipal and then UNESCO. Mosaic broken-tile benches, a salamander fountain, and a terrace over Barcelona are Gaudí's civic stage. The columns of the hypostyle hall are a stone forest. It sits on Carmel Hill, a climb. Buskers and tickets. The original plan was a garden city for the rich. The city got a playground of curves instead."});
  set("lm:sintra", {extract:"A Romanticist palace above Sintra, Portugal, rebuilt in the 1840s for Ferdinand II on the site of a monastery. Ferdinand II rebuilt a ruined monastery as a Romanticist palace in the 1840s, a collage of Moorish, Gothic, and theatrical color above Sintra. The surrounding cultural landscape is UNESCO-listed, a hill of quintas and mist. Fog and eucalyptus. The walk to the Moorish castle is another ticket. Lisbon is a train away. Byron liked it, which did not hurt. Interior rooms are a 19th-century fever dream of tiles and antlers."});
  set("lm:belem", {extract:"A sixteenth-century fortified tower on the Tagus at Belém, Lisbon, built to guard the river mouth in the age of oceanic voyages. It guarded the Tagus mouth in the age of sail, a sixteenth-century fortified tower that looks like a stone ship. UNESCO lists it with the nearby Jerónimos Monastery, where Vasco da Gama is buried. Pasteis de nata are the other pilgrimage. The Discoveries monument is a later slab of empire nostalgia. Lisbon's river is wide here, almost the sea. Cannons still point at water that now holds container ships."});
  set("lm:giza", {extract:"The tomb of Khufu on the Giza Plateau, built in the twenty-sixth century BCE. Khufu's pyramid held the height record for millennia, a tomb of about 2.3 million blocks on a plateau that is now a suburb of Cairo. It is the last standing monument of the classical Seven Wonders. Khafre and Menkaure complete the trio; the Sphinx keeps watch. Tourists, camels, and a sound-and-light show occupy the same sand. Robbers got there first by a long time. The interior is a steep, hot, empty corridor to a granite room."});
  set("lm:sphinx", {extract:"A limestone recumbent sphinx on the Giza Plateau, generally dated to Khafre. The nose was already missing in early modern drawings; blaming Napoleon's gunners is a myth. A recumbent limestone lion-man, generally dated to Khafre, with a body worn by wind and a face recarved by time. The Dream Stele of Thutmose IV sits between the paws. Restoration is a century of concrete and argument. It is smaller than the pyramids and more photographed. Sunrise is the hour of the postcard."});
  set("lm:luxor", {extract:"A temple complex at Thebes, modern Luxor, developed over many reigns. Karnak grew over many reigns rather than one, a temple city of Amun-Ra with a hypostyle hall of columns like a stone papyrus swamp. It is not in Luxor temple — that is another complex down the avenue of sphinxes. Thebes on the east bank; the Valley of the Kings on the west. Heat is a character. Hot-air balloons at dawn are the modern ritual. Hieroglyphs still take the light."});
  set("lm:tablemtn", {extract:"A flat-topped sandstone mountain overlooking Cape Town. The national park around it was proclaimed in 1998; the mountain is far older sandstone on a granite base, a mesa that defined a sea route. The cableway opened in 1929. Cloud over the top is the tablecloth. Dassies (hyrax) own the rocks. It is a small wilderness above a city of four million. Fires sweep the fynbos. The view is two oceans when the air is clean. Platteklip Gorge is the old walk."});
  set("lm:vfalls", {extract:"A waterfall of the Zambezi on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border. Livingstone named it for Queen Victoria; the local name Mosi-oa-Tunya means The Smoke That Thunders. The Zambezi drops into a basalt gorge on the Zambia–Zimbabwe line, a curtain of water that can be thin in the dry season and blinding in the wet. Devil’s Pool on the lip is a seasonal swim. Rainbows are common in the spray. The bridge is a 1905 steel arch. Both countries run parks. Mist feeds a rainforest in the gorge."});
  set("lm:kili", {extract:"A dormant stratovolcano in Tanzania and the highest mountain in Africa at 5,895 meters. Uhuru Peak is 5,895 meters, the highest in Africa, a free-standing volcanic massif with a shrinking ice cap. Most climbers use the Machame or Marangu routes; altitude, not technical ice, is the problem. Hemingway's snows are now patches. Moshi and Arusha are the gate towns. Five ecological zones from banana to crater. Porters are the industry. It is a national park and a national symbol of Tanzania, visible from the plane on a clear morning."});
  set("lm:sahara", {extract:"Steep Nubian pyramids at Meroë on the Nile in Sudan, tombs of the Kushite kingdom from about 300 BCE to 350 CE. The Nubian pyramids at Meroë are steeper than Giza's, a royal cemetery of the Kingdom of Kush, hundreds of them on a desert bend of the Nile in Sudan. Fewer tourists, more sand, and a history that textbooks skip. Camels and a small museum. The site is UNESCO-listed and under-visited. Meroitic script is still not fully read. The desert here is not empty — it is a former empire's graveyard."});
  set("lm:taj", {extract:"A white marble mausoleum at Agra on the Yamuna, commissioned by Shah Jahan for Mumtaz Mahal and completed in the mid-seventeenth century. The tomb is of Mumtaz Mahal, wife of Shah Jahan, finished about 1653 after two decades of work. White marble, four minarets, and a garden of charbagh on the Yamuna at Agra. The interior is a screen and a cenotaph; the true graves are below. Pollution yellows the stone; a restoration of mud packs is the treatment. Sunrise tickets are the ritual. The red sandstone mosque and jawab flank it. Empire built this with a tax base and a grief."});
  set("lm:redfort", {extract:"A sandstone fortress-palace in Delhi begun by Shah Jahan in 1639 when the Mughal capital moved from Agra. The walls are red sandstone; many halls were marble. Shah Jahan built it as the palace of Shahjahanabad, finished 1648. The Indian prime minister addresses the nation from its ramparts on Independence Day. Nadir Shah looted it; the British occupied it. Lahori Gate is the postcard. It is a fort that is a city plan: courts, hammam, and a mosque. Delhi's old city presses against the ditch. Evening light makes the walls look like they are still warm."});
  set("lm:gwall", {extract:"A restored Ming section of the Great Wall northeast of Beijing, with close-set watchtowers on a forested ridge. The Ming rebuilt much of the wall that visitors see; earlier walls were tamped earth. Mutianyu is a restored stretch in Huairou, generally quieter than Badaling. It is not visible from the moon with the naked eye — that is a myth. Watchtowers, steep steps, and a ridge of brick follow the mountains. It kept some people out and failed against others. Autumn color on the hills is a fine time to go."});
  set("lm:forbid", {extract:"The imperial palace of the Ming and Qing in Beijing, a walled city of halls and courtyards used from 1420 until 1924. It contains nearly 1,000 buildings, the imperial palace of Ming and Qing, finished as a complex in 1420 and closed to the public until the 20th century. The axis from Tiananmen to the rear gardens is a lesson in power and geomancy. Empty rooms, throne halls, and a lot of yellow tile. The last emperor left; the museum stayed. It is a city inside a city inside Beijing. Winter is the crowd-free season. The moat still holds water."});
  set("lm:fuji", {extract:"An active stratovolcano and Japan's highest peak at 3,776 meters. The present cone is a few thousand years old, a stratovolcano that is a national image and a UNESCO site. The official climbing season is summer only on the main routes; the rest of the year is ice and closure. Hokusai's views made it a brand before tourism. Five lakes on the north; Shinkansen views on a clear day from Tokyo, which are rare. Sacred to Shugendo and to postcard makers. The last eruption was 1707. Cherry trees in the foreground are a photographer's arrangement."});
  set("lm:tokyo", {extract:"An orange-and-white lattice communications tower in Minato, Tokyo, completed in 1958 and standing 333 meters. It was the tallest structure in Japan when it opened in 1958, 333 meters of lattice painted international orange and white for aviation. It is a television tower that became a neighbourhood mascot in Minato. Tokyo Skytree is taller now; the older tower still photographs better against cherry trees. The observatory looks at a city that rebuilt after firebombing. Foot Town at the base is a 1950s mall. It is not Eiffel, but it studied Eiffel."});
  set("lm:itsuku", {extract:"The offshore grand gate of Itsukushima Shrine on Miyajima, Hiroshima Prefecture. The shrine is rebuilt periodically in the Shinto tradition, a pier of halls on a tidal flat of Miyajima. The torii stands in the water and can be walked at low tide. UNESCO lists it. Deer wander the island, sacred and hungry for maps. The mountain behind is a holy hill. Hiroshima is a short train and ferry. Vermilion against green is the palette. High tide is the postcard; low tide is the barnacles."});
  set("lm:petronas", {extract:"Twin skyscrapers in Kuala Lumpur completed in 1998. The towers are joined by a skybridge at floors 41 and 42, once the world's tallest buildings (1998–2004), a stainless Islamic geometry of 88 floors. They are still the tallest twin towers. Kuala Lumpur's skyline starts here. Tickets for the bridge sell out. Night lighting is a civic event. They sit on a former race course. Architect Cesar Pelli's brief was: look like Malaysia. Stainless steel and glass did the rest."});
  set("lm:angkor", {extract:"A twelfth-century temple city near Siem Reap, built by Suryavarman II. It began as a Hindu temple to Vishnu, a city of water and laterite that is the largest religious monument by some measures of area. The moat is a rectangle of sky. Bas-reliefs of the Mahabharata run for hundreds of meters. Siem Reap is the gate town of hotels. Sunrise at Angkor Wat is a tripod forest. The Khmer empire's hydraulic city is still being mapped. Restoration is French, Japanese, and Cambodian in layers."});
  set("lm:marina", {extract:"A hotel and observation deck on Marina Bay in Singapore, opened in 2010. The rooftop platform is longer than the Eiffel Tower is tall, a ship of three towers on Marina Bay, opened 2010. The infinity pool is the photograph that sold the hotel. Singapore's skyline is a planned postcard from this angle. Casino, mall, and a garden of supertrees next door. Heat and air-conditioning. It is a building that is a brand. The bay is reclaimed water. National Day fireworks use it as a backdrop."});
  set("lm:burj", {extract:"A skyscraper in Dubai standing 828 meters, opened in 2010. It stands 828 meters, more than twice the Empire State, a buttressed spire of desert concrete opened in 2010. The architect, Adrian Smith, pulled the plan from a desert flower and an Islamic geometry. Most of the tower is not occupied in the usual sense — it's height as spectacle. At the top the wind and the curve of the earth are the show. Dubai Creek is a long way below. Night is when it makes sense. Records fall; this one has lasted."});
  set("lm:palm", {extract:"An artificial archipelago in the Persian Gulf off Dubai, built in the shape of a palm tree and crescent. The fronds are made of dredged sand, an artificial archipelago in the shape of a palm, begun in 2001. The breakwater is a crescent of Arabic calligraphy. Villas, hotels, and an argument about coasts and coral. From altitude it is a logo; from the road it is gates and landscaping. The Atlantis hotel anchors the crescent. Subsidence and sea-level rise are the long problem. It is a city that invented more shoreline."});
  set("lm:petra", {extract:"The rock-cut capital of the Nabataeans in present-day Jordan. The city was little known to most Europeans until 1812, when Burckhardt found it. Al-Khazneh, the Treasury, is a façade at the end of the Siq, a sandstone canyon that is the real approach. Tombs, a theatre, and a monastery up a long stair complete the site. The Nabataeans piped water through a desert trading city of incense routes. The sandstone is fragile. Flash floods in the Siq are a real hazard. Night tours use candles."});
  set("lm:istanbul", {extract:"A sixth-century church in Istanbul built under Justinian I, later a mosque and a museum, now a mosque again. Justinian's church was completed in 537, a dome that defined Byzantine space, then a mosque, a museum, and a mosque again. The minarets are later. The interior mosaic program survived lime and plaster in patches. It sits on the old acropolis of Byzantium, facing the Golden Horn. Crowds are constant. The call to prayer and the tourist audio-guide compete. It is the building that conquered and was conquered. The square is a bus park."});
  set("lm:capp", {extract:"A volcanic region of central Turkey known for fairy chimneys, underground cities, and rock-cut churches around Göreme. Fairy chimneys are eroded tuff from ancient volcanoes, valleys of pigeon houses, churches, and underground cities in central Anatolia. People have lived in the carved rock for centuries; frescoes in Göreme are a Byzantine gallery. Balloons at dawn are the modern harvest. Uchisar castle is a rock. Winter is snow on dust. It is a landscape that looks digital and is just geology plus poverty plus UNESCO. Horses still work some valleys."});
  set("lm:opera", {extract:"A performing-arts center on Bennelong Point in Sydney Harbour, designed by Jørn Utzon and opened in 1973. Utzon resigned before it was finished, a Danish architect versus a New South Wales government, and the shells still look like sails or orange segments depending on the brochure. More than a million tiles cover them. It opened in 1973, late and over budget, and became the national image anyway. Performances inside are the point that photographs forget. The harbour is the other instrument. Bennelong Point was a fort, then a tram shed. Now it is a roof."});
  set("lm:harbour", {extract:"A steel through-arch opened in 1932, linking the north and south shores of Port Jackson. The arch was built from both shores and joined in the middle in 1932, a steel through-arch that still carries rail, road, and pedestrians. It used millions of rivets. Photographers famously sat on the arch at lunchtime during construction. It is older than the Opera House and just as important to the skyline. Climbing the arch is a later visitor activity. Ferries still pass underneath. Painting it is a continuous job."});
  set("lm:uluru", {extract:"A large sandstone inselberg in Australia's Northern Territory, sacred to the Anangu. It is sacred to the Anangu, who asked visitors not to climb; the climb closed in 2019. A sandstone inselberg in the red center, it changes color with the sun — the description is accurate. The surrounding land is a national park jointly managed. Waterholes and rock art line the base walk. Flies can be thick in warm months. Nights are cold. The sky is the rest of the park."});
  set("lm:gbrier", {extract:"Cairns is the main city for the northern Great Barrier Reef, a system of thousands of reefs and islands off Queensland. The reef is made of billions of tiny coral animals, the world's largest coral system, visible from orbit as a different color of sea. Bleaching events are the crisis. Cairns is the gate for the outer reef tourist machine: boats, snorkels, and a debate about love and damage. It is not one reef but thousands. Cyclones break and rebuild. The lagoon is a pasture of seagrass and dugong. UNESCO has warned. The water is the point and the problem."});
  set("lm:hobbiton", {extract:"A film set and farm in the Waikato of New Zealand used as Hobbiton, left standing as a village of hobbit holes. The first film set was built in 1999 and later rebuilt as a permanent village on a sheep farm near Matamata. Tours are timed; the green hill is still a working pasture. It is not Tolkien's Oxfordshire — it is Waikato farmland with round doors. Merchandise and a mill that never mills. Fans cry; others wonder at the power of a movie to invent a pilgrimage. The party tree is a real tree. Rain makes it more Shire, which is frequent."});
  set("lm:milford", {extract:"A fjord in Fiordland on New Zealand's South Island, with Mitre Peak rising from rain-fed water. Mitre Peak rises more than a mile from the water, a postcard of Fiordland that is actually a drowned glacial valley, not a Norwegian fjord in geology. Piopiotahi is the Māori name. Sandflies are the tax. The road in is a tunnel and a pass; boats do the rest. Rainfall is among the highest on earth, which is why the waterfalls work. It is a national park of wet beech and kea. The sound is quiet when the boats leave."});
  set("lm:redeemer", {extract:"A reinforced-concrete statue of Christ on Corcovado above Rio de Janeiro, dedicated in 1931. Lightning has struck the statue many times. It is 30 meters of soapstone on a 8-meter pedestal, Christ the Redeemer on Corcovado, opened 1931, arms open to a city of bay and favelas. The train up the mountain is the old way. Cloud often eats the head. It is the emblem of Rio because the pose is a blessing and a brand. Interior stairs exist. The view is Sugarloaf, the lagoon, and a lot of traffic. Art Deco folds in the robe."});
  set("lm:sugarloaf", {extract:"A granite and quartz peak at the mouth of Guanabara Bay in Rio de Janeiro, reached by cable car. The first cable car opened in 1912, a granite monolith of 396 meters at the mouth of Guanabara Bay. The name is a loaf of sugar in a mold, a colonial metaphor. Two stages of gondola. Climbers treat the faces as a gym of slabs. It is smaller than Corcovado and better as a ship-from-the-sea. Sunset is the booking. The military still has a footprint on the Urca shoulder. Granite, sea, and a city that climbs every slope."});
  set("lm:iguazu", {extract:"Waterfalls of the Iguazu River on the Argentina–Brazil border. The name is often said to mean great water. The falls are a system of cataracts on the Argentina–Brazil line, with Garganta del Diablo the U-shaped main drop. National parks occupy both sides; the Argentine walkways get closer, the Brazilian get the panorama. Coatimundis are bold around picnic tables. Spray is a climate of its own. The river is a Paraná tributary. UNESCO lists both banks. Flood and drought change the width."});
  set("lm:machu", {extract:"A fifteenth-century Inca estate on a ridge above the Urubamba in Peru, abandoned after the Spanish conquest and widely known after 1911. It was unknown to the Spanish during the conquest, a royal estate in a cloud forest saddle, brought to outside attention by Hiram Bingham in 1911 — though local farmers were not lost. Terraces, temples, and a tourist train from Cusco. Permits cap the day. Llamas are photogenic staff. The Inca trail is a different, longer arrival. Stones fit without mortar. Morning mist is the postcard. It is a ruin that still works as a village of guides."});
  set("lm:chichen", {extract:"A large Maya city in Yucatán. The equinox shadow looks like a serpent on the stair of El Castillo, a temple to Kukulcán, a pyramid of the Maya in Yucatán. Cenotes, a ball court, and a lot of iguanas. It is a restored site; some of the restoration is imaginative. Tour buses from Cancún are the river. The name is a well of the Itza. Sound-and-light at night. It is not the only Maya city; it is the famous one. Heat is dry compared with the coast."});
  set("lm:teoti", {extract:"A pre-Aztec city northeast of Mexico City, known for the Pyramids of the Sun and Moon and the Avenue of the Dead. The city's builders are still not named with certainty; the Aztecs found it already ruinous and called it the place of the gods. The Avenue of the Dead aligns the pyramids of the Sun and Moon, a grid of a city that may have held 100,000 people. It is 25 miles northeast of Mexico City. Climbing the Sun pyramid is now restricted. Obsidian and a fire in the 7th century. The scale is imperial. Dust and vendors at the gate."});
  set("lm:cancunm", {extract:"The cliff castle of the Maya port of Tulum on the Caribbean coast of Quintana Roo, looking over the sea from a walled town. Tulum was a late Maya port facing the reef, a small castle on a cliff above turquoise water that photographs better than it explains. El Castillo is the postcard; the rest is walls and tourists in swimwear. It is not Chichen; it is a beach ruin. Cruise and hotel corridor of the Riviera Maya. Iguanas again. The site is fragile and crowded. Morning is the hour. The Caribbean is the other temple."});
  set("lm:cn", {extract:"A concrete communications and observation tower in Toronto, completed in 1976 and 553 meters tall. It held the title of world's tallest free-standing structure for 30 years after 1976, a concrete needle with a main pod at 351 meters. The revolving restaurant is a 1970s future. Glass floor in the lower observation deck. Toronto's skyline grew around it. Night lighting can be a maple leaf. It is still the city's letterhead. Lake Ontario is the south view. Edgewalk is a later product for people who like harnesses."});
  set("lm:banff", {extract:"A glacial lake in the Valley of the Ten Peaks in Banff National Park, Alberta. Banff was Canada’s first national park, established in 1885 as a railway spa that became a valley of hotels and elk. Moraine Lake’s rockpile and turquoise water are among the most photographed views in the Rockies, and parking is now managed. The lake is glacially fed and changes color with rock flour. Both black and grizzly bears live here. The town sits in a UNESCO park. Winter is for skiing; summer is the busy season. The Continental Divide is a short drive."});
  set("lm:peyto", {extract:"A glacier-fed lake in Banff National Park, seen from Bow Summit on the Icefields Parkway. Rock flour from glaciers colors the water a blue that looks edited. The viewpoint on the Icefields Parkway is one of the most photographed in the Rockies, a short walk from a bus pullout. No town. The lake is a finger in a hanging valley. Crowds at midday; empty at first light. The glacier that feeds it is receding, which will change the color eventually. Wind on the platform. It is a postcard that is still a place."});
  set("lm:moai", {extract:"Monolithic human figures carved from tuff on Easter Island by Rapa Nui people. About 900 statues were carved, most from Rano Raraku tuff, the quarry where unfinished giants still sleep in the slope. Most ahu face inland; the living watched the ancestors watch them. Collapse, missionary era, and a 20th-century re-erection. The island is Rapa Nui, far from Chile in miles and closer in politics. Horses, a few roads, and a runway. Carving stopped for reasons still argued: ecology, war, rats. The hats are red scoria."});
  set("lm:torres", {extract:"A park in Chilean Patagonia named for three granite towers of the Paine Massif, with lakes and glaciers around them. The granite towers are horn-like spires above glacial lakes, the emblem of a 1959 park in Patagonian Chile. Wind is the climate. The W trek is the industry. Guanacos, pumas, and a lot of weather that cancels boats. Puerto Natales is the gate. The massif is a batholith carved by ice. It is not the end of the world, but you can see it from here. Torres in dawn light are orange for a few minutes."});
  set("lm:easter2", {extract:"The largest ahu on Rapa Nui, with fifteen restored moai facing inland from the coast near the quarry of Rano Raraku. A tsunami had knocked the row down; it was re-erected in the 1990s, fifteen moai on Ahu Tongariki, the largest ahu, with the quarry behind them. Sunrise is the photograph. The ocean at their backs is a long fetch of empty. Restoration used cranes, which the original engineers did not. It is the postcard of the island now, more than a single moai. Spray on a windy day. The road is a loop."});
  set("lm:moscow", {extract:"A church on Red Square in Moscow, commissioned by Ivan IV and completed in 1561, with clustered onion domes. Ivan IV ordered St. Basil's after the capture of Kazan, a 1561 cluster of chapels that looks like a fire and is a church. The interior is smaller than the exterior promises. Red Square is the stage; the Kremlin wall is the other side. Stalin almost demolished it. The colors were once plainer. It is not the cathedral of the patriarch — that moved. Tourists circle it like a campfire. Winter makes it a toy."});
  set("lm:hermitage", {extract:"The former residence of Russian emperors on Palace Square in Saint Petersburg, now part of the Hermitage Museum. Catherine II began the art collection that fills the Winter Palace and its neighbours, more than a million works, a museum that is also a Baroque city. The Jordan staircase is a coup of marble. Siege of Leningrad: the collection was evacuated; the building suffered. It faces the Neva. You cannot see it in a day. Gold and parquet and a fatigue of masterpieces. Petersburg light is pale and long in June."});
  set("lm:kremlin", {extract:"A fortified complex on the Moskva River that has been the seat of Russian power for centuries, with cathedrals and palaces inside the walls. The brick walls visible today are mostly fifteenth century, an Italian-engineered fortress that is still the seat of the Russian presidency. Cathedrals of the old tsars share the hill with palaces and a theatre of power. Lenin is outside, on the square. Red stars on the towers replaced eagles. It is a working citadel, not only a museum. The river is the south. Tours are routed. Bells and a clock that the country sets itself by."});
  set("lm:nordkapp", {extract:"A high cliff on Magerøya in northern Norway, long presented as a northern extremity of Europe. The plateau is a well-known cape, though a nearby knoll is slightly farther north. A globe monument and a visitor center stand on the cliff. The midnight sun appears in summer; polar night in winter. German forces fortified it in the Second World War. Magerøya is the island; a tunnel reaches it. The cliff is real, and the weather is rarely gentle. Atlantic and Arctic air meet here."});
  set("lm:trolltunga", {extract:"A horizontal tongue of rock about 700 meters above Ringedalsvatnet near Tyssedal in Vestland, Norway. The name means troll tongue, a rock ledge over a lake in Vestland. The hike is about 28 kilometers round trip and became widely known through photographs. The ledge is granite left by ice. Odda is the old industrial gateway. Summer is busy; shoulder season can be icy. Weather closes the trail without much warning. The view is from the air or from the tongue itself."});
  set("lm:geiranger", {extract:"A branch of Storfjorden in western Norway, known for steep farms, waterfalls, and deep water between Geiranger and Hellesylt. The fjord is part of a UNESCO listing with Nærøyfjord, a deep, steep drowned valley of farms on ledges and waterfalls named the Seven Sisters. Cruise ships are the economy and the problem. Geiranger village is tiny. Eagle Road switchbacks. Avalanches in winter. The water is green with rock flour. It is Norway's postcard fjord because the walls work from a ship's deck. Rain is frequent. The abandoned farms are the older story."});
  set("lm:hallgrim", {extract:"A Lutheran church in Reykjavík designed by Guðjón Samúelsson and completed in 1986. The tower is 74.5 meters, a concrete church whose façade is meant to recall basalt columns, finished in 1986 after a long build. It dominates Reykjavík because the city is low. The tower gives a view of colorful roofs and a grey sea. A statue of Leif Erikson stands outside, a gift from the United States. Organ concerts are held here. It is Lutheran and modern. Wind on the observation deck is not a figure of speech."});
  set("lm:gulfoss", {extract:"A two-stage waterfall of the Hvítá in southwest Iceland, dropping into a canyon on the Golden Circle. It sits on the Golden Circle with Þingvellir and Geysir, a two-step drop of the Hvítá into a canyon, mist and a trail that ices in winter. A woman, Sigríður Tómasdóttir, is credited in story with helping save it from a hydro scheme. In winter it can freeze at the edges into sculpture. Rainbows in the spray. Tour buses, again. The name means golden falls. It is loud. The canyon is the surprise after the lip."});
  set("lm:blue", {extract:"A geothermal spa in a lava field near Grindavík, Iceland. The spa opened to the public in 1992, runoff from the Svartsengi geothermal plant, milky with silica, in a lava field on the Reykjanes peninsula. It is not a natural wild pool — it is industrial water made luxurious. Masks of white mud. The 2020s eruptions and a town evacuation at Grindavík put it on pause more than once. It is Iceland's most famous bath and a brand. The water is warm when the wind is not."});
  set("lm:borobudur", {extract:"A ninth-century Mahayana Buddhist monument in Central Java: terraces and stupas covering a hill with narrative reliefs. It was buried under ash and jungle until the nineteenth century, a Buddhist stupa-mountain of the Sailendra period, about 825 CE, the largest of its kind by some measures. Terraces of reliefs, a walk of pilgrimage to the top buddhas in latticed stupas. Yogyakarta is the gate. Restoration is Dutch and Indonesian. Sunrise from the hill is a ticketed ritual. Islam is the majority religion now; the monument is a national one. Stone, fog, and a lot of steps."});
  set("lm:prambanan", {extract:"A ninth-century Hindu temple compound in Central Java dedicated chiefly to Shiva, with a tall central tower. A major reconstruction followed a 2006 earthquake. The central spire is dedicated to Shiva, a 9th-century Hindu temple compound of tall prangs in a plain near Yogyakarta, not far from Borobudur and a different theology. Ramayana ballet is performed on some nights. Many smaller shrines are still piles. It is a reconstruction you can see the joints in. The skyline of spires is the Java of textbooks. Vendors and a park of dry grass."});
  set("lm:komodo", {extract:"An Indonesian island in East Nusa Tenggara, habitat of the Komodo dragon, in a national park with Rinca and Padar. The national park was founded in 1980 for a lizard that can kill a deer, on islands of dry forest and pink beaches. Labuan Bajo on Flores is the usual gate. Dragons are the product; the park is also coral and a conservation argument about tourists and goats. Boats, heat, and a ranger with a forked stick. They are the largest lizards on earth. The island is not a zoo, though it can feel like one at the feeding sites, which are now restricted."});
  set("lm:bali", {extract:"A sea temple on a rock off the southwest coast of Bali, one of seven sea temples around the island. The temple is one of seven sea temples around the island, a shrine on a rock in the tide west of the tourist south. Sunset is the crowd; priests still work the ceremonies. Tanah Lot means land in the sea. It is photogenic and crowded, which is the Bali problem in miniature. Offerings of flowers and plastic. The rock is volcanic. Waves are the other congregation. Ubud is a different Bali; this is the postcard one."});
  set("lm:ha-long", {extract:"A bay in the Gulf of Tonkin, Quảng Ninh, Vietnam, filled with limestone islands and sea caves. The name means descending dragon, thousands of limestone islands in a bay of the Gulf of Tonkin, a UNESCO seascape of junks and karst. Overnight boats are the product. Hạ Long city is the coal-and-tourist shore. The islands are hollow with caves. It is crowded and still astonishing. Fishing villages on rafts have been moved. The best light is haze, which is frequent. Kayaks in the lagoons are the quiet version."});
  set("lm:angkor2", {extract:"The state temple of Jayavarman VII at Angkor Thom, known for stone faces on its towers and reliefs of Khmer life and war. Jayavarman VII built it as his state temple, a Buddhist mountain of face-towers at the center of Angkor Thom. The faces are often identified with the king or with Lokesvara, serene and repeated. It is not Angkor Wat — it is stranger, a pile of stone and jungle that the jungle almost won. Ta Prohm is the tree-root cousin nearby. Restoration pulled some of the ruin back. The faces follow you. Heat in the enclosures."});
  set("lm:everest", {extract:"The highest mountain on earth at 8,849 meters, on the Nepal–Tibet border. The first confirmed ascent was Hillary and Tenzing in 1953. The peak sits on the Nepal–China border, 8,849 meters, a traffic problem and a morgue in the climbing season. Namche and the Khumbu are the economy of porters, lodges, and oxygen. The icefall is the technical horror of the south side. Climate change opens and closes routes. It is a pyramid that became a brand. Most of the world only sees it from a plane window or a poster."});
  set("lm:potala", {extract:"A fortress-palace in Lhasa, winter residence of the Dalai Lamas from the seventeenth century until 1959. The present palace rose in the seventeenth century as the winter seat of the Dalai Lamas, a white and red ziggurat above Lhasa, now a museum in a political situation that the architecture cannot explain. A thousand rooms, butter lamps, and a view of a city that has grown around it. UNESCO. Altitude is 3,700 meters; stairs hurt. The Chinese flag is on the roof. Pilgrims still circuit. Photography rules change. It is a fortress of a faith."});
  set("lm:jeju", {extract:"A tuff cone on the east shore of Jeju Island, South Korea, formed by a hydrovolcanic eruption and climbed for sunrise. The crater is a tuff cone from a hydrovolcanic blast, Seongsan Ilchulbong, sunrise peak, a UNESCO piece of Jeju's volcanic field. Sunrise from the rim is a Korean New Year tradition. The island is south of the peninsula, a different climate of tangerines and wind. Haenyeo women still dive. The cone is grassy and steep. Tour buses at dawn. It is not a mountain so much as a bowl that holds the sun for a minute."});
  set("lm:nseoul", {extract:"A communications and observation tower on Namsan in central Seoul, opened in 1980. It stands on Namsan in the center of the basin, a tower with locks of love on the railings and a cable car from a city of 9 million. Opened 1980, a communication spire that became a date. The view is Seoul's endless apartment ridges and, on a rare day, mountains. Night is better. It is not Taipei 101; it is a hill with a needle. The old city walls pass nearby. Smog is a character. The locks are so many they have to be cut."});
  set("lm:taipei101", {extract:"A skyscraper in Taipei completed in 2004, 508 meters to the tip. A giant tuned mass damper hangs in the upper floors, a gold sphere that sways to keep the tower still in typhoons and quakes. It was the world's tallest from 2004 to 2010, a pagoda of stacked sections, 508 meters. The elevator is one of the fastest. Night markets at the foot. It looks like a bamboo stalk by intent. Taipei's basin is the rest of the view. Fireworks on New Year. The damper is a tourist exhibit, which is honest engineering."});
  set("lm:hkpeak", {extract:"The high point on Hong Kong Island at 552 meters. The Peak Tram opened in 1888, a funicular to a view of a working harbor. Victoria Peak is not the highest point on Hong Kong Island, but it is the one with the deck. Fog can erase the city. Night is the reason many people go: a density of light that defines the harbor. Colonial villas, a shopping mall, and many steps share the hill. Reclamation has narrowed the harbor since the old photographs. The view still works."});
  set("lm:moai2", {extract:"An ahu on Easter Island with seven moai. These seven figures at Ahu Akivi face the sea, unlike most ahu, in a restoration of 1960. Oral tradition says they look toward the ancestral homeland. They stand inland rather than on a cliff, following a different logic. The island’s interior is pasture and scoria. Fewer visitors come here than to Tongariki. Sunset lights their faces. Restoration used archaeology and a good deal of lifting."});
})();
